WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.888
The painter example initially knows how to draw
each concrete shape. Adding a triangle

00:00:04.888 --> 00:00:09.775
requires another branch or loop in the painter,
spreading the variation into a client.

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:14.208
Introducing a Shape operation moves drawing
behavior behind a shared contract.

00:00:14.208 --> 00:00:19.043
The painter can then iterate over shapes without
naming every concrete type, making the

00:00:19.043 --> 00:00:20.989
supported extension point explicit.

00:00:21.208 --> 00:00:26.293
Open–closed design protects stable behavior from
selected kinds of change; it does not

00:00:26.293 --> 00:00:31.614
forbid editing code or demand inheritance for
every extension. Composition, strategies and

00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:36.640
registration can serve the same purpose. Adding
a shape may still require creation or

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.532
configuration changes elsewhere.

00:00:38.708 --> 00:00:43.860
The key question is whether the abstraction
matches the expected variation, and whether its

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:49.011
contract is sufficient for new implementations
to work without special cases in the client.

00:00:49.011 --> 00:00:53.879
After introducing Shape.draw, must every file
remain unchanged when Triangle is added?

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:59.316
No. The painting loop can remain stable, while
Triangle and its creation or registration

00:00:59.316 --> 00:01:00.922
still need implementation.

00:01:00.922 --> 00:01:06.492
Substitutability concerns behavior visible to
clients. If a client works under a supertype

00:01:06.492 --> 00:01:11.938
contract, replacing an object with a subtype
should not invalidate those expectations. A

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:16.642
subtype must not demand stronger preconditions
or provide weaker guarantees.

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:21.515
Phrase this through implications: every input
accepted by the supertype must remain

00:01:21.515 --> 00:01:26.565
acceptable, and the subtype’s successful outcome
must satisfy the supertype’s promised

00:01:26.565 --> 00:01:26.976
result.

00:01:27.142 --> 00:01:32.246
The mutable Rectangle example promises
independently adjustable width and height.
Square

00:01:32.246 --> 00:01:37.292
breaks that promise by changing both dimensions
in a setter, so a client expecting area

00:01:37.292 --> 00:01:42.164
fifty receives one hundred. This is a contract
problem rather than a statement about

00:01:42.164 --> 00:01:42.686
geometry.

00:01:42.686 --> 00:01:48.158
The unmodifiable List example needs different
care: Java documents optional mutators, so an

00:01:48.158 --> 00:01:53.450
exception can be permitted by the declared
contract. A client requiring mutation needs a

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:58.862
stronger capability guarantee. Does the
mathematical fact that every square is a
rectangle

00:01:58.862 --> 00:02:01.568
prove mutable Square is a behavioral subtype?

00:02:01.686 --> 00:02:08.002
No. The programming interface’s mutation
promises, not just geometric membership,
determine

00:02:08.002 --> 00:02:09.182
substitutability.

00:02:09.186 --> 00:02:14.581
Interface segregation focuses on what each
client needs to depend on. A broad interface can

00:02:14.581 --> 00:02:19.917
mix reading, writing and event handling even
when many clients need only one role. Smaller

00:02:19.917 --> 00:02:22.407
role interfaces make capabilities clearer.

00:02:22.407 --> 00:02:27.542
An adapter with default no-op callbacks may
reduce implementation effort, but it does not

00:02:27.542 --> 00:02:30.716
erase the conceptual breadth of the original
interface.

00:02:30.907 --> 00:02:36.749
Dependency inversion places policy behind stable
abstractions instead of binding it directly

00:02:36.749 --> 00:02:42.338
to implementation details. JDBC illustrates
application code using a database-facing API

00:02:42.338 --> 00:02:47.673
implemented by drivers. The boundary can
localize many implementation changes, while

00:02:47.673 --> 00:02:52.055
behavioral differences and contract changes may
still affect clients.

00:02:52.055 --> 00:02:56.970
Together, these principles encourage focused
contracts owned by the needs of the caller,

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:01.717
with concrete implementations replaceable only
to the extent that they preserve those

00:03:01.717 --> 00:03:06.855
contracts. Does adding an interface guarantee
callers are unaffected by every implementation

00:03:06.855 --> 00:03:07.246
change?

00:03:07.246 --> 00:03:12.707
No. Changes in observable behavior, contract or
supported capabilities can still affect

00:03:12.707 --> 00:03:15.845
callers even when method signatures remain
stable.
